1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:05,822
Look up on a dark autumn
night, far from city lights.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:13,713
Near the constellation Andromeda, there's a
faint smudge. Most people never notice it.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:19,059
It isn't a cloud. It isn't a
star. It's another galaxy —

4
00:00:19,059 --> 00:00:22,168
a spiral of perhaps a trillion suns.

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:30,993
If your eyes could gather enough light, it would
stretch across the sky, about six full Moons wide.

6
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:37,008
The light you're seeing left it
two and a half million years ago —

7
00:00:37,008 --> 00:00:39,148
before our species existed.

8
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:47,806
It is the farthest thing most of
us will ever see with our own eyes.

9
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:53,196
And it is coming toward us.

10
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:08,228
In 1912, astronomer Vesto Slipher measured
its light — and found it shifted toward blue.

11
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:10,753
Andromeda was approaching.

12
00:01:10,753 --> 00:01:16,512
Today we know it's closing at about a
hundred and ten kilometres every second.

13
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:24,161
Four hundred thousand kilometres every
hour. Roughly the distance to the Moon.

14
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:30,436
For decades, astronomers thought
a head-on collision was certain.

15
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:36,292
But measuring its sideways
drift is extraordinarily hard.

16
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:46,391
Recent studies using the Hubble and Gaia spacecraft disagree: some
give it a coin-flip chance of merging within ten billion years;

17
00:01:46,391 --> 00:01:49,880
newer work puts it closer to ninety percent.

18
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:52,906
So let's follow the version where it comes.

19
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:05,249
In about two billion years, it
would no longer be a smudge.

20
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:10,963
Night after night, nothing
would seem to change.

21
00:02:10,963 --> 00:02:14,117
But over millions of years, it would swell.

22
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:24,239
Its spiral arms, its dark lanes of dust, its
glowing core — all visible to the naked eye.

23
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:29,967
Some four billion years from
now, it could fill the sky —

24
00:02:29,967 --> 00:02:33,390
a second galaxy, hanging over the horizon.

25
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:43,279
Then our own Milky Way would begin to bend, pulled
out of shape by the gravity of its neighbour.

26
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:53,017
Two galaxies, a hundred billion stars
and more each, falling into one another.

27
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:07,269
And yet, almost no stars would collide.
The space between them is simply too vast.

28
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:11,465
But the clouds of gas would.

29
00:03:11,465 --> 00:03:16,879
They'd crash together and ignite,
lighting the sky with newborn stars.

30
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:26,207
Over billions of years, the two spirals would
settle into one great, glowing elliptical galaxy.

31
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:31,859
Astronomers already have
a name for it: Milkomeda.

32
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:40,315
Our Sun would likely survive — perhaps flung
to a new orbit, far from where it is today.

33
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:44,136
But no one on Earth will see it.

34
00:03:44,136 --> 00:03:49,643
Long before then, the Sun will have
grown too bright for oceans, or for us.

35
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:55,645
Someone, somewhere, may be
watching. It won't be us.

